<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jarrow</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jarrow is a town on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its monastery of St Paul, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-biscop">Benedict Biscop</a> in the early 680s and joined with Wearmouth, was a centre of Northumbrian learning where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> lived and wrote, and the dedication stone of its church, dated 685, survives. In modern times Jarrow was a shipbuilding town, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow-march">Jarrow March</a> of 1936 against unemployment became a symbol of the Depression.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jarrow.jpg" alt="Jarrow" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xaphire at Dutch Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jarrow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jarrow lies on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a> in South Tyneside, about 5 km from the east coast and 10 km north of Sunderland. It was historically part of County Durham. Its name comes from the Gyrwe, an Anglian tribe whose name means fen dwellers. Jarrow was an ancient parish of seven townships and was incorporated as a municipal borough in 1875; the borough was abolished in 1974 and absorbed into South Tyneside.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Monastery of St Paul at Jarrow, with Wearmouth part of the twin foundation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Monkwearmouth–Jarrow Abbey</a>, was the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">the Venerable Bede</a>, whose works include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastical-history-of-the-english-people">Ecclesiastical History of the English People</a>. The two houses became a centre of learning, with the largest library north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, largely through the travels of their founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-biscop">Benedict Biscop</a>. In 692 Ceolfrid commissioned three copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-amiatinus">Codex Amiatinus</a>, the world's oldest complete Latin Bible, which Ceolfrid was taking to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ii">Pope Gregory II</a> when he died on the way. It was later at Monte Amiata in Tuscany and since 1786 has been in the Laurentian Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, who had plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> in 793, attacked Jarrow in 794, and in 875 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfdan-ragnarsson">Halfdan Ragnarsson</a>'s force sacked the monasteries at Tynemouth and Jarrow. Henry VIII later dissolved the monastery.</p><p>Jarrow stayed a small town until heavy industry arrived. Charles Mark Palmer set up a shipyard there in 1852, and about 1,000 ships were built at Palmer's, which also made the first iron screw collier, John Bowes, and the first modern cargo ship. At times the yard employed up to 80 per cent of the town's working population. It closed in 1933 after National Shipbuilders Securities bought it to cut capacity, though the yard was relatively efficient and modern.</p><p>The Depression left Jarrow, in the words of Life, cursed. In 1936 the town was the starting point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow-march">Jarrow March</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> against unemployment; its MP Ellen Wilkinson wrote of these events in The Town That Was Murdered (1939). Historians doubt how effective such marches were, though some evidence suggests they stirred interest in regenerating distressed areas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> revived shipbuilding, and the last shipyard closed in 1980.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jarrow's built-up area had 29,470 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The church of St Paul stands on the monastery site, incorporating its ruins, and beside it is Jarrow Hall, a museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s life and times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jarrow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Benedict Biscop founded Wearmouth in 674 and <b>Jarrow</b> in the early 680s, with books, glaziers and masons brought from Gaul and Rome, and Abbess Hild ruled the double monastery at Whitby.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 710 King Nechtan son of Der-Ilei wrote to Abbot Ceolfrith of Wearmouth and <b>Jarrow</b> asking for arguments on the dating of Easter and for masons to build a stone church, and in 717 he expelled the clergy of Iona who refused the Roman Easter.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Restored sandstone fragments found in <b>Jarrow</b>, dating from 118 or 119, record Hadrian's wish to keep the empire &quot;intact&quot;, which had been imposed on him via &quot;divine instruction&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/work/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
